                      │
             ┌────────┼────────┐
             ▼        ▼        ▼
          Volume A  Volume B  Volume C
11- You normally don't create an Engine Image yourself. Longhorn creates/manages it.
You create a Backing Image only when you have a specific use case for starting volumes from pre-existing disk content.

12- Image Engine indicates: Which version of the storage engine (V1 or V2) should this volume use.

13- You create a backing image when you have some data that you want multiple Longhorn volumes to start from.

14- System backup = backup of longhorn itself

15- Data is replicated across the different nodes of the Longhorn cluster = HA

16- Rancher K8s Upgarde refers to upgrading the Kubernetes version of your downstream cluster managed by Rancher

17- trim feature is to reclaim storage from volume. = also with recurring job you can automate the trim operation. you will need to reclaim free storage 
when you delete some files or data and the volume size doesn't get reduced. 

18- for volume resize feature you need to be using the longhorn storage class and allowVolumeExpansion set to true. size can be increased but never decreased. 

19- data locality = Longhorn leverages the concept of scheduling pods on the same node where their associated Longhorn volumes are located.

20- when using strict-local data locality mode in Longhorn, the number of replicas for a volume should be set to 1.
strict-local setting enforces that Longhorn keeps exactly one single replica of the volume on the identical node 
where the workload (pod) is actively running.

21- to update the data locality for an existing volume you should know on which node your pod is running, go to the volume and chnage
the data locality and number of replica attach the volume to that node.

22- With best-effort data locality, every time your pod issues a write operation, that write goes to all replicas at the exact 
same millisecond, including the local one.

23- With strict-local the pod will issue write/read operation to only 1 volume on the same node of the pod. 

24- Data Locality Settings ExplainedIn distributed storage systems for Kubernetes (like Longhorn), 
Data Locality determines how storage replicas are placed relative to the Pod using them.
Strict-Local: Guarantees that one storage replica is always kept on the exact same node as the running Pod. 
It ensures 100% local reads and writes, minimizing network latency.

Best-Effort: The system tries to keep a replica on the Pod's node.
If that node lacks space or fails, the Pod still runs, and all storage traffic goes over the network to other nodes.

Disabled: Replica placement completely ignores where the Pod is running. 
Replicas are distributed across the cluster based purely on disk space and scheduling tags.

Scenario 1: Strict-Local and Pod ReschedulingIf you use Strict-Local and your Pod gets rescheduled to a different node:
The volume will attach to the new node.A new replica is immediately created on this new node.
Data begins syncing over the network from the old replica to the new local replica.
The old replica is deleted once the new local replica becomes fully healthy, maintaining your set replica count.

The Catch: While the new replica is syncing, your Pod will experience network latency because it has to read and write data
across the network from the old node.

Scenario 2: 3 Nodes, 2 Replicas, Best-Effort vs. DisabledYou have 3 nodes (Node A, Node B, Node C) and a volume with 2 replicas. 
Your Pod schedules onto Node A.
With Best-Effort:Placement: The system forces 1 replica onto Node A (the Pod's node).
The 2nd replica is placed on either Node B or Node C.
Performance: Excellent. Your Pod reads and writes directly to the local replica on Node A without network lag.

If Node A fails: The Pod can reschedule to Node B. 
Since it is "Best-Effort," the Pod will successfully start on Node B and talk to the remaining replica over the network while it tries to build a new local replica.

With Disabled:
Placement: The 2 replicas are placed randomly across the 3 nodes (e.g., Node B and Node C). 
Node A might get no replica at all.
Performance: If Node A has no replica, every single read and write operation from your Pod must travel over the cluster network to Node B or C.
If Node A fails: The Pod moves to another node, but its storage performance remains dictated by random network placement.

25- you can specify the data locality in a strogeclass that ou create manually and use it for PVC/PV creation.

26- Replica Auto-Balance in Longhorn helps maintain data redundancy and load distribution in the cluster by
automatically redistributing volume replicas as needed when a new node added in the cluster. This ensures
that the storage infrastructure remains efficient, resilient, and well-balanced, making it a valuable feature for
managing data in a distributed storage environment.
Longhorn offers three choices for the global replica auto-balance setting:

Disabled (Default): In this mode, no automatic replica auto-balancing occurs.

Least-Effort: This option prompts Longhorn to balance replicas with minimal redundancy adjustments. For
instance, on your node-1 there are 4 off-balanced replicas when a new node node-2 is added, a volume with
four off-balanced replicas will only rebalance one replica.

Best-Effort: Selecting this mode instructs Longhorn to make the best effort to balance replicas evenly for
optimal redundancy. For example, on your node-1 there are 4 off-balanced replicas after adding a new node
node-2, a volume with four off-balanced replicas will have two replicas rebalanced.

27- recplica auto balance in longhorn vs replica node level soft anti-affinity disbaled
Longhorn Replica Auto Balance automatically moves or rebalances replicas when cluster topology changes, 
whereas disabling Replica Node Level Soft Anti-Affinity strictly prevents placing multiple replicas of the same volume onto the same node.

Replica Auto BalancePurpose: Moves or recreates replicas to achieve an even distribution across available nodes and zones when a new node joins or volume replica counts change.
Action: Actively evaluates cluster capacity and placement optimization (using modes like least_effort or zone/disk pressure rules).

Replica Node Level Soft Anti-Affinity (Disabled)
Purpose: Controls whether multiple replicas for the same volume are allowed to reside on the same physical node.
Action: When disabled (hard anti-affinity enforced), Longhorn blocks any node that already holds a healthy replica from receiving another replica for that same volume.

28- Replica Auto Balance can be applied at following places:
– Setting up global replica auto-balance by selecting the preferred
option in General settings, once saved it applies to new volumes
without affecting existing ones
– Configure Replica Auto Balance for an existing volume, by
selecting the specific volume, and use Update Replicas Auto
Balance option to customize auto-balancing settings
– To schedule Replica Auto Balance via a storage class, start by
creating a new storage class with defined parameters, and
create a PVC manifest file using this storage class

29- Prometheus does not guess what it needs to monitor. 
It absolutely requires a target defined by a ServiceMonitor (or PodMonitor) object to know where to collect (scrape) the metrics 
and allow the execution of PromQL queries.

when Service Monitor is created and configured to montiro the longhorn-manager pods, Prometheus immediately starts collecting Longhorn metrics, 
and Longhorn-specific dashboards are automatically injected into Grafana on their own."

29- Within General Settings, configure the Pod Deletion Policy When Node is Down

– Set it to delete-both-statefulset-and-deployment-pod

– This policy prompts Longhorn to force delete terminating pods of
StatefulSet/Deployment on inactive nodes, releasing associated volumes for swift replacement by Kubernetes
fter force deleting, Kubernetes will detach the Longhorn volume and spin up replacement pods on a new node.

30- Recover from node failure mean : pod is created on node 3, volumes are on same node, node 3 goes down, Pod Deletion Policy When Node
is Down is enabled so pods will get detached from volumes on that node, fter force deleting, Kubernetes will detach the Longhorn
volume and spin up replacement pods on a new node.

31- Prometheus is responsible for evaluating and firing the alert, while Alertmanager is responsible for receiving, grouping, and sending it.
Prometheus:
Evaluates your alert rules using data from your metrics.
Detects when a condition becomes true (e.g., CPU usage is over 90%).
Changes the alert state to firing.Sends the firing alert to Alertmanager over the network.

Alertmanager:
Receives the alert from Prometheus.
Removes duplicates and groups similar alerts together.
Applies silences and inhibitions (rules to block certain alerts).
Sends the final notification to your chosen destination, like Slack, PagerDuty, or email.

32- Only data that overwrites information in older snapshots is included in a new snapshot.

33- to restore a snapshot you need to scale down the pod first this will detach the volume, the select volume and attach it to any node and select maintenance
and then revert the snapshot, then detach the volume again, and scale the pod to 1: it will be attached to the pod. 

34-Longhorn presents three distinct scenarios when deleting a
snapshot:
– Deleting a snapshot with zero bytes has no impact on other snapshots in the
chain.
– When deleting a snapshot containing data, Longhorn automatically executes a
snapshot purge, merging the data into the next snapshot in line.
– The latest snapshot in Longhorn cannot be deleted because of the snapshot
purge process, which involves merging the content of a deleted snapshot with
the next one, that cannot occur without a subsequent snapshot .
● The next entity in line is the live volume, actively in use and incompatible with the
deleted snapshot

35-Allow Recurring Job While Volume is Detached:  if volume is detached I can't do a snapshot myself but this option automates taking a snapshot
even when the volume is detached. this is usefull if you want to restore the data from detached volume in another volume. 

36- volume attached/detached means attached/detached to/from node. (not workload)

37- Object Storage: Explicitly breaks up the file at the software layer and assigns pieces to specific disks alongside massive metadata.
Block Storage: Choops data into anonymous raw blocks. A single hard drive might keep them together, 
but modern enterprise/cloud block storage always scatters blocks across multiple disks to maximize performance

38- The Step-by-Step Retrieval ProcessWhen you click "Open Video.mp4", this exact sequence happens in milliseconds:
The Request: Your application asks the File System for Video.mp4.
The Lookup: The File System looks at its structural index table. 
It sees that Video.mp4 is mapped to an exact list of anonymous, raw block numbers: [Block 102, Block 508, Block 900].
The Fetch: The File System tells the Storage Controller: "Give me Blocks 102, 508, and 900."The Gathering: The Storage Controller
checks its hardware map to see exactly where those numbers live physically (e.g., Block 102 is on Disk 1, Block 508 is on Disk 2, 
Block 900 is on Disk 3). It pulls them from the physical disks simultaneously.
The Assembly: The blocks are delivered back to the File System in raw, binary form.
The File System presents them back to your computer as a single, seamless file.

39- It is recommended to schedule recurring backups for volumes to ensure a consistent data protection strategy.
In the absence of a backupstore, recurring snapshots serve as a viable alternative.
a backup in Longhorn strictly requires a BackupStore. You cannot create a Longhorn backup without configuring one.
A snapshot does not require a BackupStore.A snapshot is completely self-contained. It is created and stored locally on the exact 
same nodes and disks that are already running your live Longhorn volumes.
